            type: "text",
            id: "j-l13-b2",
            body: "A **declaration** is hoisted: the name exists before the line, value and all, so `add(2, 3)` works even when the call comes first. An **expression** is a function built inside a value, and nothing is available until the assignment runs \u2014 calling `double(4)` above its `const` throws. Every arrow is an expression, so arrows follow that rule even though they are written to look like declarations. The practical habit: define before you use, and order stops mattering."
