            body: "A loop that builds a new array inside itself is the most common shape in beginner JavaScript, and the array methods replace every one of them. Each takes a function, calls it once per item, and hands the results back in a form suited to what you were trying to do. Once you can name which one you want, the loop disappears. Two things are true of all of them: the array you called the method on is **unchanged**, and the result is a brand-new array that shares nothing with it. That is why `map` and `filter` chain so well \u2014 each one leaves the original intact for the next, and there is never a moment where a half-finished change is sitting in your data. The exception is `sort`, which does mutate."
